  "description": "Perform fractionally strided transposed convolution projecting spatial input activations into larger spatial canvas.\n\nTransposed convolution corresponds to the backward pass adjoint operation of standard convolution, enabling learnable spatial upsampling.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{Y}_{i:i+K_h, j:j+K_w} += X_{i, j} \\cdot \\mathbf{K}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Initialize output canvas of size (H + Kh - 1, W + Kw - 1)\n2. Multiply kernel matrix by each input scalar and accumulate into overlapping output regions\n3. Return expanded spatial output rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list representing upsampled transposed convolution output.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: 2D Transposed Convolution\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nImages have unique spatial properties: neighboring pixels are strongly correlated, and visual patterns (like edges, corners, or textures) appear anywhere in an image. Computer vision algorithms use spatial inductive biases like translation equivariance and local receptive fields to process image grids efficiently. **2D Transposed Convolution** operates directly on spatial coordinates or bounding box geometries.\n\nTransposed convolution corresponds to the backward pass adjoint operation of standard convolution, enabling learnable spatial upsampling. In this problem, the goal is to: perform fractionally strided transposed convolution projecting spatial input activations into larger spatial canvas.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Y}_{i:i+K_h, j:j+K_w} += X_{i, j} \\cdot \\mathbf{K}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Initialize output canvas of size (H + Kh - 1, W + Kw - 1)\n2. **Phase 2**: Multiply kernel matrix by each input scalar and accumulate into overlapping output regions\n3. **Phase 3**: Return expanded spatial output rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Spatial Locality**: Sliding spatial filters across multi-channel feature maps aggregates contextual clues across local neighborhoods.\n* **Object Detection Pipelines**: Bounding box metrics (such as IoU and Non-Maximum Suppression) reconcile duplicate candidate detections into clean, final predictions.\n* **Data Augmentation**: Geometric transformations expose models to diverse spatial viewpoints, significantly improving generalization to unseen test images.",
